"VERDICT\n\nThe committee's judgment is split, and deliberately so. For the muon collider: yes \u2014 this concept should be selected for continued R&D. The requirement there is cell-level background rejection at 30\u201360 ps on few-to-tens-of-GeV EM deposits in an extreme radiation field, and the DSB:Ce capillary channel's photostatistics-limited scaling with no resolved constant term, at a cell size already matched to ECAL granularity, is a credible trajectory toward that spec \u2014 the current 2\u20134x shortfall at few-GeV deposits is a stated R&D target, not a disqualifier. For FCC: a qualified yes, but only as reframed. As a high-energy EM shower time-stamp (FCC-hh) or an embedded timing/position layer (FCC-ee), the extrapolated 25\u201335 ps at 50\u2013100 GeV is competitive with CMS-ECAL-class precedent \u2014 if the constant term cooperates, which is unmeasured. As a calorimeter, and as a 5\u201310 ps vertexing device, it is not selectable on this evidence. Separately: the concept earns the next tranche; this prototype is a TRL 3\u20134 validation of a light-collection channel, not a system demonstration, and several of its headline numbers must be re-derived before any external review.\n\nWHY\n\nFive strengths were decisive. First, the timing result itself: DSB:Ce at 133 \u00b1 5 ps at 9 GeV reference-included, with a median-consistent intrinsic ladder of roughly 111/87/75 ps at 5/7/9 GeV compatible with pure photostatistics. Second, the constant term was shown to be engineerable, not module-intrinsic \u2014 swapping only the capillary set at fixed W/LYSO stack halved it. Third, error accounting rare at this readiness level: the diagonal-difference estimator independently recovered the known ~110 ps reference floor and, in failing on the bright channel, discovered correlated capillary timing \u2014 the physics floor for multi-fiber averaging, found before a tower was ever built. Fourth, exemplary systematics forensics: the saturation prosecution, the gain-ladder diagnosis, the invalidation of the team's own MIP calibration anchor, and the conversion of the EJ-199 failure into a quantified contaminant fingerprint. Fifth, the demonstrated bottleneck is the lab DAQ, not the detector.\n\nFive gaps were equally decisive. The flagship 75 ps is a quadrature subtraction of near-equal numbers below the DAQ's own jitter floor, with the reference validated only on LuAG runs before an MCP voltage change; relatedly, the quoted 79 ps constant term sits below the validated 107\u2013114 ps reference and is unphysical \u2014 it must not be quoted again, and the published intrinsic ladder mixes estimators. Radiation hardness \u2014 the project's raison d'\u00eatre \u2014 was not measured, and the down-select is inverted: the timing winner is the least radiation-qualified material in the stack. Intrinsic energy resolution was never measured at any point; every \u03c3/E is position-smearing dominated, linearity holds only to a \u00b115 % gain envelope with no monitoring plane, and the calorimeter case is simply unmade (honestly so \u2014 the site never overclaims). Dynamic range already fails at 11 GeV while target showers are 30\u2013100x brighter, and 9 GeV is the maximum clean energy, leaving the decision-critical constant term with no lever arm. Finally, the record itself: registry entries contradicting DAQ metadata, merged input files with no generating code, a containment-floor inconsistency that changes the published EJ-199 intrinsic numbers, and stale figures \u2014 reproducibility needs a cleanup pass.\n\nOPEN QUESTIONS\n\nRanked: (1) Does 75 ps at 9 GeV survive an independent downstream reference of \u226420\u201330 ps jitter on a non-DRS4 path, and does the LuAG-validated common mode apply to the DSB1 runs? (2) What is the intrinsic constant term at 20\u2013150 GeV, measured directly at SPS rather than by subtraction \u2014 is it ~20 ps or ~60 ps? (3) Does DSB:Ce retain light yield and timing after MGy-class gamma and 10\u00b9\u2075\u201310\u00b9\u2076 neq/cm\u00b2, and does LuAG overtake it post-irradiation? (4) Is the correlated capillary timing physical shower fluctuation or common-mode readout \u2014 separable with a downstream reference, shuffled cabling, and an impact-position map? (5) What is intrinsic \u03c3/E at 1\u20139 GeV with impact-position tagging, decomposed against an absolute photoelectron scale? (6) What is the event-by-event longitudinal-fluctuation term of shower-max-only sampling, from simulation validated against the measured Longo migration? (7) What pixel and dynamic-range budget delivers linearity at 30\u2013100x today's light?\n\nTHE ROAD\n\nFirst, extract what this dataset still holds: re-derive the intrinsic ladder with one estimator and retire the 79 ps figure; fix the DiagDiff EJ-199 floors and re-log those points; replace two-point trend solves with weighted fits; compare LG-path pulse shapes across channels to settle whether tile light is actually being collected; reconcile registry against FINDINGS, add checksums and provenance for the derived files, and regenerate the two pre-house-style figures before anything is shown externally. Second, bench work: the dark-pulse photoelectron calibration, per-batch incoming-material QA (absorption/emission plus decay-time fingerprint), and a dynamic-range design note identifying the front-end ASIC and clock-distribution path. Third, launch the irradiation campaign now, on real capillary assemblies of both DSB:Ce and LuAG:Ce including couplings and SiPMs \u2014 it is the longest-lead item and it, not beam data, decides the down-select. Fourth, the next beam test: SPS H2/H4 at 20\u2013150 GeV, an independent downstream time reference, a tracker or hodoscope, LED/laser pulser with temperature logging and frozen bias, a 3x3 module matrix, rescoped low-gain range, and lead-converter runs for e/\u03c0 versus energy. Passing that gate would make this a rare energy-plus-timing device; failing it still leaves a fundable shower-max timing layer."